ere white  men who or white women who thought that  involving the police for example was the  best possible situation which doesn't  make sense if you know if you're already  a marginalized person who's  disproportionately targeted by  harassment and we live in a country  where the police can be used because  stuff like swatting where people get  your home address call in a fake bomb  threat of EPs happening at your house  hoping that a SWAT team will go to your  house that you'll think someone's  breaking and it resists and that they'll  shoot you dead like saying that that  institution that has you know had  numerous issues with violence towards  marginalized people being the solution  to a problem that marginalized people  disproportionately face is tone-deaf and  waiting for a lot to catch up and  pushing off all the responsibility is  like onto oh well we just need better  laws or oh we definitely more arrest is  negligent it's not and it's like every  time I would be in these rooms usually  with other people who you know were  brought in to have that viewpoint  alongside ours would have the same  conversations over and over having to  tell these basic facts like no this is  not relevant to my experience with  marginalized person and  did you even think about like dead  naming policies which is a specific sort  of violence usually directed at trans  people or they find like pretended  student information and threatened or  harassed them with it's like most a lot  of tech companies weren't even thinking  about that being a form of harassment it  just looks like a name to them so it's  like how could that be Erasmus it's not  threat so that's one of the reasons why  I like more perspective is needed  Silicon Valley is a major issue we're  very insular those people making our  laws are very fit there's not at times  diversity there either so having it  having everybody who sort of makes these  decisions and not have these  perspectives is frustrating and I can't  